          "question": "What does guidance describe as secondary prevention after a heart attack?",
          "answer": "If you are having chest pain, pressure, or trouble breathing now, call your local emergency number or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it passes.\n\nFor your question, the guidance I could retrieve, *Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis* (major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary), defines secondary prevention as the measures used for someone who already has established coronary artery disease, such as a previous heart attack, a procedure to open the arteries, or visible narrowing on a scan. Their purpose is to reduce the chance of a further event.\n\nThe passage I retrieved does not list which measures are included, so I can't tell you what they are from these sources. A clinician can explain which measures apply to a particular person and why.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
